    "created_at": "2026-08-29T00:33:23Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-09-09T23:45:23Z",
    "body": "Same issue here — Zed 1.19.2 on Linux, dev container via `devcontainer.json`.\n\nAfter rebuilding the container (`--remove-existing`), all previous agent threads\nvanished from the sidebar, and opening them from Threads History failed silently\n(window flash, `docker exec <old_id> … No such container` in `Zed.log`). It looks\nexactly like lost history — but none of the content was actually gone: everything\nwas still on the host in `~/.local/share/zed/threads/threads.db`, and I recovered\nall threads from there (analysis and recovery below done with AI assistance).\n\n## What actually breaks — and what doesn't\n\n- **Thread content is never at risk.** All messages live in\n  `~/.local/share/zed/threads/threads.db` (`threads` table; `data` is\n  zstd-compressed JSON per thread). After a rebuild, every pre-rebuild thread is\n  still intact there — verifiable by decompressing the blobs.\n- What breaks is only **reattachment**: `sidebar_threads.remote_connection` in\n  `~/.local/share/zed/db/0-stable/db.sqlite` still carries the old\n  `container_id`, so pre-rebuild threads (a) disappear from the sidebar and\n  (b) silently fail to open — the stale options fall through to\n  `connect_remote`, i.e. `docker exec` against a container that no longer exists.\n- The net UX is that users reasonably conclude their history is **lost**, when\n  it is all on disk and merely unreachable through the UI. That perception is\n  why this reads as a much worse bug than it is.\n\n## Why it happens\n\nThe identity of a dev container that is stable across recreation is\n`(name, remote_user)` — `name` from `devcontainer.json`, `remote_user` from\nconfig. But `RemoteConnectionIdentity::Docker` also includes `container_id`,\nwhich is a fresh ID on **any** recreation: explicit rebuild, `docker rm` + up,\nor a host reboot that brings the container back as a new one.\n`same_remote_connection_identity` then rejects the stored thread's connection\nagainst the live one. Still present on current `main`\n(`crates/remote/src/remote_identity.rs` keys on\n`docker:{remote_user}@{name}:{container_id}`, checked at ee3b5558c5).\n\n## Notes on the SQL workaround\n\nRewriting the stale `container_id` in `sidebar_threads.remote_connection`\nworks, with two gotchas:\n\n1. **Zed must fully exit — not just the window.** `ThreadMetadataStore` loads\n   the table into memory once at process startup and never re-reads it (no\n   file watch; `reload()` only runs from init migrations in\n   `crates/agent_ui/src/thread_metadata_store.rs`). Any DB edit made while the\n   process is alive is invisible until restart, and my Zed process had been\n   running for days across window closes.\n2. `archived=1` rows stay hidden from the sidebar even with a correct id, and\n   the panel matches `folder_paths` exactly — multi-root threads\n   (`/mnt/... + /workspaces/x`) won't show in a single-root `/workspaces/x`\n   window.\n\n## Suggested fixes\n\n1. Drop `container_id` from `RemoteConnectionIdentity::Docker` (identity =\n   `(name, remote_user)`), keeping `container_id` in `RemoteConnectionOptions`\n   for the live connection only — as proposed above.\n2. Stop `get_backup_project_name` from falling back to `container_id` when\n   `devcontainer.json` has no `name` — use a fixed string. Otherwise unnamed\n   dev containers silently keep the ephemeral identity and reintroduce the bug\n   through the back door.\n3. **Self-heal instead of the manual SQL workaround:** on (re)connecting to a\n   dev container, if a stored identity matches on `(name, remote_user)` but\n   differs on `container_id`, rewrite the stored id in place\n   (`sidebar_threads.remote_connection` and the corresponding\n   `remote_connections` row — cf. #55864, same stale-id shape). The first\n   reconnect after a rebuild then repairs the metadata; no\n   \"quit Zed, back up the DB, run SQL\" dance.\n4. Surface the failure: when thread resolution hits \"No such container\", show\n   a toast (\"couldn't reconnect to the dev container this thread was created\n   against — it may have been rebuilt\") instead of a silent window flash. That\n   alone turns \"my history disappeared\" into \"click to reattach\".\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
